#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c4d2228-2456-536c-87f5-c05f03ac1532" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents an Inverse Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1039e812-9a88-5b9b-b551-269c2393b23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517522} {:answer/id #uuid "763b9117-6856-5174-865d-697d35865dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ does not vary inversely with $x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517712} {:answer/id #uuid "aef8d086-e3c3-5c2f-b73f-df730fbb213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517817} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b86d47-b504-5381-aaca-f8c9b8bcb39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517837} {:answer/id #uuid "f07b6d5a-a5a6-55bc-bf86-f6c9b061a44a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef8d086-e3c3-5c2f-b73f-df730fbb213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with a constant of variation $k=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517817} :answer-field/id #uuid "f89a1044-5822-5030-9b03-184a93ebec0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11ab4bcc-ff94-5b73-9790-5edd61c08193" :question/math-academy-id "q-115488" :question/problem "Consider the table below. Which of the following is a true statement about the relationship between $x$ and $y?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $20$ | $0.5\\cdot 20=10$ |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $2\\cdot 5=10$ |\n| $4$ | $2.5$ | $4\\cdot 2.5=10$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=10.$ Therefore, $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "482e3ce6-1938-5bbe-a0d3-2c44213c7746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff8e18-e1e0-569c-b239-ea2d323c2f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517506} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3f1cdb-62a7-5446-bfc0-9c5b8ecff4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517570} {:answer/id #uuid "37c06ed4-4dac-5a25-bb1d-90e1da0780f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517598} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5ad2d6-7a2b-58fa-9b9e-b5cfbe4b0f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517610} {:answer/id #uuid "72cc3a7f-05b3-50d0-8c4f-4aedc32143ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72cc3a7f-05b3-50d0-8c4f-4aedc32143ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517708} :answer-field/id #uuid "d155659c-c17e-5eca-b3d0-23cecfde70bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf708341-904e-56d2-b929-e74c203d19a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115589" :question/problem "![](images/96/9674072fe2d9f831635dfd2994b97ea6e9603dd576586f80e4b1b2e6a2373564.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between $x$ and $y.$ Given that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x,$ find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5 & =k \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation that relates $y$ and $x$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2})}{x}=\\frac{5}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we also have that $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ when $x=a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2a} \\\\ 10a & =10 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e239c25-66f9-5ea7-ba3c-5599966cd89b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Data Point Given Another Data Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e2c107-9d5e-504f-af80-17ffca1d2a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e2c107-9d5e-504f-af80-17ffca1d2a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517858} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f79e97-d630-5908-a05a-ee3b6a7eb854" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c37fb77-7c99-58f3-b32e-45223dbee74b" :question/math-academy-id "q-217248" :question/problem "Suppose that $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ Then, if $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ when $x=12.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{k}{12} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 12 & =k \\\\ 9 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{9}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =9÷(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
